<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>ExampleApp</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleSignature</key>
	<string>????</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Cleartext HTTP to the host this app is being developed on, and to
	  nothing else. The Android side of this exact trade is
	  android/app/src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml, which names
	  the same hosts and records the same weighing; the two files exist to
	  say the same thing in each platform's own vocabulary.

	  WHY IT EXISTS. The level 2 facade run (packages/example-app/level-two)
	  stands a throwaway Matrix homeserver and a matrix-nio counterparty up
	  on the developer's own machine, on the loopback interface, and the app
	  talks to both. There is no certificate to trust for a container that
	  exists for ninety seconds, and there should not be: everything here is
	  loopback traffic between two processes on one machine. An iOS
	  simulator sees the developer's machine at 127.0.0.1, so that and
	  localhost (Metro) are the only names this app needs.

	  WHY IT IS NOT NSAllowsLocalNetworking="true". That key is all-or-
	  nothing for unqualified names, .local domains and every IP address,
	  IPv4 and IPv6, RFC1918 included; since iOS 17 ATS applies to
	  IP-address connections again and the key is what re-opens them all.
	  This app uses exactly one IP address and one host name, so it names
	  them instead of the class. The two names below cannot leave the
	  machine.

	  WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, AND WHY THE APP DOES NOT RELY ON IT ALONE. On
	  iOS 13 through iOS 16 ATS does not apply to IP-address connections at
	  all, so no key here could have restricted them; on those versions the
	  closure is the probe itself. The app's plan-fetch URLs are
	  platform-scoped (src/levelTwoTransport.ts): an iOS build never probes
	  the Android emulator's 10.0.2.2 alias, which on a physical iPhone
	  attached to a 10/8 or LAN network is an ordinary routable RFC1918
	  address a host could answer from, handing this demo app a homeserver
	  address and an access token. Nothing of the user's is at risk,
	  because this app holds nothing of the user's, but the exposure was
	  real and this file is where the two halves of the closure meet.

	  RULE FOR ANYONE ADDING A NAME BELOW: a name here is a name this app
	  may speak unencrypted HTTP to, on every network it is ever attached
	  to, not only on the developer's own machine. Weigh it as 10.0.2.2 is
	  weighed above. A real product ships no such file.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
		<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
		<dict>
			<key>localhost</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
				<true/>
			</dict>
			<key>127.0.0.1</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
				<true/>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</dict>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>RCTNewArchEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
